Q5324 | power | north | The Pratihara dynasty, also called the Gurjara-Pratiharas or the Pratiharas of Kannauj, was a prominent medieval Indian dynasty. | The Pratihara kings ruled a large part of northern India from the city of Kannauj.
Q866014 | religion | himalaya | Kedarnath Temple, in the Garhwal Himalaya near the Mandakini River, is a Hindu temple and one of the twelve jyotirlingas of Shiva. | Kedarnath is a temple of the god Shiva high in the Himalaya. Pilgrims climb up to it.
Q4848728 | arts, religion | east | Baitala Deula is an 8th-century Hindu temple in the Khakara style of Kalinga architecture, dedicated to the goddess Chamunda. | Baitala Deula is a temple to a goddess, built in Odisha about 1,300 years ago.
Q668412 | power, religion | east | The Pala Empire was founded in the late 8th century when the chiefs of Gauda elected Gopala as king. | The chiefs of Bengal chose a man named Gopala to be their king. His family, the Palas, ruled for centuries.
Q856691 | power | deccan | The Rashtrakuta Empire ruled large parts of the Indian subcontinent between the 6th and 10th centuries. | The Rashtrakuta kings of the Deccan ruled one of the biggest empires of their time in India.
Q1268562 | arts, religion, technology | deccan | The Kailasa temple is the largest of the rock-cut Hindu temples at the Ellora Caves in Maharashtra: a megalith carved into a cliff face, called "the climax of the rock-cut phase of Indian architecture". | The Kailasa temple at Ellora was not built from blocks. The whole temple was carved out of one cliff of solid rock.
Q877155 | power, trade | south | The Chera dynasty, known from the Sangam period, ruled over parts of what is now Kerala in southern India. | The Chera kings ruled the land that is now Kerala, on India's south-west coast.
Q1634624 | science, religion | gangetic | Vikramashila was a Buddhist monastic university in what is now Bihar, founded by the Pala king Dharmapala. | Vikramashila was a great Buddhist university in Bihar, founded by King Dharmapala.
Q2039087 | power | central | The Chandela Empire existed from the early 9th century to the 14th century, with its power base in Jejakabhukti. | The Chandela kings ruled central India for about 500 years.
Q6806806 | power, trade | south | The Chola Empire, often called the Imperial Cholas, was a medieval sea-based empire in southern India ruled by the Chola dynasty. | The Chola Empire was a great kingdom of southern India that ruled the seas.
Q1617123 | power | himalaya | The Utpala dynasty, founded by Avantivarman in 855, was a Hindu dynasty that ruled Kashmir in the 9th and 10th centuries. | King Avantivarman started a new family of rulers in Kashmir in the year 855.
